{"name":"Dead Sea Mud","hex":"#3a403b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dead","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["No longer living; deceased.","Devoid of living things; barren."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Exactly.","Very, absolutely, extremely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dead"},{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"},{"term":"mud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mednyj Aleut."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture of water and soil or fine grained sediment.","A plaster-like mixture used to texture or smooth drywall."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dead Sea","blocks":[{"text":"The Dead Sea, also known by other names, is a landlocked salt lake bordered by Jordan to the east, the West Bank to the west and Israel to the southwest. It lies in the endorheic basin of the Jordan Rift Valley, and its main tributary is the Jordan River."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Dead_Sea_beach_00.JPG/330px-Dead_Sea_beach_00.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dead_Sea"},{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"},{"title":"Mud","blocks":[{"text":"Mud is loam, silt, or clay mixed with water. Mud is usually formed after rainfall or near water sources. Ancient mud deposits hardened over geological time to form sedimentary rock such as shale or mudstone. When geological deposits of mud are formed in estuaries, the resultant layers are termed bay muds. Mud has also been used for centuries as a construction resource, mostly for houses, and also as a binder. An Old English word for it was fen, now in most dialects referring to a type of wetland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg/330px-2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mud"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Dead_Sea_Galilee.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_Galilee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Dead_Sea_Galilee.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_Galilee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Satellite photograph, showing the location of the Dead Sea east of the Mediterranean Sea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADead_Sea_Galilee.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg/1280px-Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Jordanian shore of the Dead Sea, showing salt deposits left behind by falling water levels","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADead_Sea%2C_Jordanian_Shore.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg/500px-ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg/1280px-ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg/500px-ISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"NASA photo showing depth of the Dead Sea basin (slightly below center). The Mediterranean Sea is on the right, with the Suez Canal visible connecting it to the Red Sea on left (slightly above center).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AISS-55_Nile_River_delta%2C_Egypt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg/1280px-Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg/500px-Dead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Halite deposits (and teepee structure) along the western Dead Sea coast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADead_Sea_Halite_View_031712.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG/500px-SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG/1280px-SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG/500px-SaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pebbles cemented with halite on the western shore of the Dead Sea near Ein Gedi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaltCementedPebblesDeadSea.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Halite_Dead_Sea_Beach_Pebbles.JPG/500px-Halite_Dead_Sea_Beach_Pebbles.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=